# Generated by Next.js, and its contents depend on whether `next dev` or `next build` ran last —
# which makes it output, not source. Tracking it meant every production build (and therefore every
# e2e run, since the suite now builds) left `git status` dirty, and PROCESS.md's "a test suite never
# writes into the working tree" rule was paid for with a defect that survived two reviews.
# turbo.json makes `typecheck` depend on `build` so the file exists before anything reads it.
apps/web/next-env.d.ts

# The unattended runner's working files (scripts/run-epics.sh, docs/AUTONOMOUS.md).
# All four are machine-local by design: a run log, a stop switch, a pid, and the resume
# point for an epic in flight. `*.log` above already covers the run log. None of them is a
# repository artifact, and an epic commit must never sweep one up — `docs/epics/RELEASE-DUE.md`
# is deliberately NOT here, because that one is for Soroush to read.
/STOP
/.run-epics.pid
docs/epics/.run-state.json
docs/epics/.run-state.json.corrupt
docs/epics/.run-outcome.json

# The operator's own session prompt, kept in the repository root for convenience.
# Not a repository artifact: it is one person's instructions to one agent, it carries no SPDX
# header (so `reuse lint` would fail on it), and an epic commit running `git add -A` will otherwise
# sweep it up — which is exactly what happened once, on 2026-09-16.
PROMPT_CONTINUE

# Soroush's working icon file, kept in the repository root while it is being chosen.
# Same reasoning as PROMPT_CONTINUE above: it carries no SPDX header, so `reuse lint` fails on it,
# and `license-gate.mjs` scans untracked-but-not-ignored files too. Left untracked and unignored it
# made `node scripts/gates.mjs ci` refuse to start, so EPIC-901, EPIC-900 and EPIC-032a all ran the
# gate with `--allow-dirty` — a flag that quietly means "one file was not tested".
#
# **Ignoring it is not the same as deciding about it.** If this icon becomes a real asset it needs a
# home under a package, a licence header or a REUSE.toml entry, and this line removed.
app-icon.jpg

# Agent skills installed from a URL by `npx skills add` (2026-09-23, Stripe's docs skills).
# `.agents/skills/` holds them and the other four are symlink trees one editor each reads.
#
# **Ignored rather than tracked, and that is about the gates rather than about taste.** Seventy-six
# untracked files with no SPDX headers fail `reuse lint`; `pnpm binary-files` and `pnpm dead-code`
# both scan untracked files; and `node scripts/gates.mjs ci` refuses a dirty tree outright. Tracking
# them instead would mean vendoring a third party's prose into a repository that licence-lints every
# file it carries.
#
# `skills-lock.json` is the installer's own lockfile. Ignored with them: pinning the set is only
# worth doing if the set is tracked, and it is not.
.agents/
.devin/
.junie/
.windsurf/
skills-lock.json
# **`.claude/skills/` and not `.claude/`**: a project `.claude/settings.json` or an agent
# definition is this repository's own and should be trackable. Only the symlink tree is not.
.claude/skills/
